        "elapsedMs": 17029,
        "text": "Absolutely—since you’ve been using **Adobe Premiere Pro**, I’d start with these resources:\n\n### Free tutorials\n- **[Adobe Premiere tutorials](https://helpx.adobe.com/premiere-pro/tutorials.html)** — A reliable starting point for cutting clips, working with audio, adding titles, and color correction. Choose lessons that match your software version.\n- **[Premiere Gal](https://www.youtube.com/@PremiereGal)** — Practical Premiere tutorials covering editing workflows, captions, effects, and audio.\n- **[Justin Odisho](https://www.youtube.com/@JustinOdisho)** — Focused walkthroughs for Premiere effects, transitions, and color work. Great for trying one technique at a time.\n- **[This Guy Edits](https://www.youtube.com/@ThisGuyEdits)** — Especially useful for the *creative* side: pacing, choosing performances, and knowing when to cut—not just which buttons to press.\n\n### Structured courses\n- **LinkedIn Learning** — Look for a recent *Premiere Pro Essential Training* course. It’s subscription-based, but some public libraries provide free access.\n- **Blackmagic Design’s [DaVinci Resolve training](https://www.blackmagicdesign.com/products/davinciresolve/training)** — Free videos, books, and practice files if you’d like to explore another editor, particularly for color grading. Many editing principles transfer to Premiere.\n\n### A book worth reading\n- **_In the Blink of an Eye_ by Walter Murch** — A short, thoughtful introduction to why cuts work emotionally and how editing shapes a story. It’s about craft rather than software.\n\n**My suggested starting project:** Make a 60–90-second video from footage you already have. First focus on a clear sequence, then clean up the audio, and finally use **Lumetri** to match exposure and white balance between shots. That’ll teach you more than watching lots of tutorials without applying them.\n\nAre you most interested in storytelling, color grading, or effects?"
      },
